{"timestamp":"2026-01-15T21:57:41.038Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_reasoning","text":"**Noting Dockerfile regeneration pending**\n\nThe generated Dockerfile lacks the updated usermod changes since createProject hasn't rerun after template updates. The recommended approach is to instruct the user to recreate or rebuild the project to regenerate files with the new template applied."}}
{"timestamp":"2026-01-15T21:57:41.039Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_reasoning","text":"**Summarizing Docker build issue and codex auth fix**\n\nThe Docker build failed because the base image already has UID 1000, so useradd without `-o` fails; the template now uses `-o` to allow reuse, but the generated Dockerfile hasn’t updated yet—recreating the project after rebuilding will regenerate it properly. The codex auth sync skipped due to the `.orch` directory being root-owned and not writable; I updated the project config and compose setup to fallback automatically to a shared writable codex auth path under `.docker-git/secrets/codex`, which avoids login prompts. \n\nNext steps: the user should rebuild the docker-git package (already done), restart the server, and run recreate to regenerate the Dockerfile. If `.orch` remains root-owned and causes permission issues, they should verify and fix ownership manually (e.g., with `ls -la` and `chown`), though I couldn’t verify this here. The changed codex resolution logic is in `effect-template/packages/docker-git/src/server/http.ts`. \n\nSOURCE: n/a"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-01-15T21:57:41.039Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"reasoning","summary":[{"type":"summary_text","text":"**Noting Dockerfile regeneration pending**\n\nThe generated Dockerfile lacks the updated usermod changes since createProject hasn't rerun after template updates. The recommended approach is to instruct the user to recreate or rebuild the project to regenerate files with the new template applied."},{"type":"summary_text","text":"**Summarizing Docker build issue and codex auth fix**\n\nThe Docker build failed because the base image already has UID 1000, so useradd without `-o` fails; the template now uses `-o` to allow reuse, but the generated Dockerfile hasn’t updated yet—recreating the project after rebuilding will regenerate it properly. The codex auth sync skipped due to the `.orch` directory being root-owned and not writable; I updated the project config and compose setup to fallback automatically to a shared writable codex auth path under `.docker-git/secrets/codex`, which avoids login prompts. \n\nNext steps: the user should rebuild the docker-git package (already done), restart the server, and run recreate to regenerate the Dockerfile. If `.orch` remains root-owned and causes permission issues, they should verify and fix ownership manually (e.g., with `ls -la` and `chown`), though I couldn’t verify this here. The changed codex resolution logic is in `effect-template/packages/docker-git/src/server/http.ts`. \n\nSOURCE: n/a"}],"content":null,"encrypted_content":"gAAAAABpaWLNsbldyITJfl4_R1IL40ijllBqLosKB7u-Zw3st-XoKl87hynngebln7wT1Rj0iuL5KXVtyQlqkVaF3p1bJZSWSYODSOH0PXFuNgpf78TysJw5uW6gENZnDrcid4F9gCcrcSMuRjBidcnMzLQW9egj7_PgIoBrEH5Q0e1L1brGEjMVAniGvM8WQz-6urFWBluSOJqFp50I4kcu3683jMVM6xk-YTIR6ampoXgBrniY_h2XjiXIOhjtgmAQYAVgO-Op4FmZWpGtihvg1cI-vXkm3LcFCQ-rXiKSfhA4FCzuBTc_8ezWarAM418Rhhl8F7V7IS5lp93V7GD7SKONhIfA2GZAqtbEHrrRZgRzO6hOd1kjZa_C8xbFwMMlR98lcjpVoCendRLWPC5ta1W5RP1VlskhFJ5sgD1DIxCdOs2VMpvIP1rDKrxPzSr5Hb6tyDoi8KYBwLbuj7WfaAbX